/* flex-grow: 1 added here, on .k2-slider-main below, and on the drag-container's own parent -
   without an explicit stretch rule the slider was sizing to some Unity-default width instead of
   filling its row, leaving a visible gap on the right before the "// SECTION" header bars above
   it (which DO span full width), so header and slider read as two different widths. */
.k2-slider {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

.k2-slider > VisualElement {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    height: 26px;
    border-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: 3px;
    align-self: auto;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/* label_row (the label+value pair) ends up as a direct VisualElement child of main_slider
   itself whenever label-on-top is false (see K2Slider.cs's setLabelPos, which inserts it into
   main_slider instead of into "this" in that case) - the ">VisualElement" rule right above this
   one was written for main_slider's real input container, but it unintentionally caught
   label_row too, since label_row is *also* a direct VisualElement child in that layout. That
   handed label_row an equal flex-grow:1 share of the row, squeezing the actual track/dragger
   down to roughly half width - Max Throttle is the one that showed it, since it's the only
   label-on-top="false" slider in current use. Locking label_row back to a content-sized, non-
   growing box (via its own id, which beats the class+type selector above) so the whole rest of
   the row's width goes to the track like it should. */
.k2-slider > #label_row {
    flex-grow: 0;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}

.k2-slider #unity-drag-container {
    /* Back to overflow: hidden (its original value) - a diagnostic swap to visible didn't change
       anything (every slider still showed no dashes with the clip removed), so clipping wasn't
       the actual mechanism either. See #unity-tracker below for where this landed. */
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-left: 0;
}

/* Retro/astromech pass, matching the stock RCS Thruster reference panel's sliders: a dashed
   line track instead of a solid black bar, and a small rectangular handle instead of a circular
   knob.
   This used to be a background-image referencing the base Redux/KerbalUI theme's own dash.png
   (Packages/uitkforksp2.controls/Assets/Theme/Sprites/dash.png) via a USS url(). Four rounds of
   troubleshooting that (a hidden->visible container transition, the reference itself dropping on
   relayout, sub-pixel height rounding, and this container's own overflow clipping it) each got
   ruled out empirically via Ksp2-2.log - resolvedStyle consistently reported everything as it
   should be (right sprite, correct non-fractional size, Visible, opacity 1) and the dashes still
   didn't render in-game. That means the failure is happening below what resolvedStyle can even
   see - somewhere in this game's embedding actually painting a tiled background-image from a
   package-sourced sprite - not something fixable by tuning more USS properties. K2Slider.cs now
   draws the dashes itself with generateVisualContent (plain Painter2D line segments, no texture
   involved at all), so background-image/tint/repeat/size are dropped here - height stays, since
   the C# draw call reads this element's resolvedStyle.height for the line's thickness. */
.k2-slider #unity-tracker {
    background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0);
    /* Explicit "none" matters here, not just omitting the property: KerbalUI.uss (the stock Redux
       theme, loaded before this file) has its own rule for .unity-base-slider--horizontal ...
       .unity-base-slider__tracker that sets a background-image to this SAME dash.png, just tinted
       a lighter blue (rgb(119, 152, 204) vs our rgb(110, 120, 140)). Our old #unity-tracker rule
       always won that property via ID-selector specificity regardless of whether it actually
       rendered, so this stock rule never showed - but the moment this rule stopped declaring
       background-image at all (switching to the Painter2D draw in K2Slider.cs), the cascade fell
       through to KerbalUI's declaration instead, which showed up as a lighter dashed line ghosting
       behind our darker hand-drawn one. Declaring none here re-blocks it explicitly. */
    background-image: none;
    visibility: visible;
    height: 3px;
    margin-top: -1px;
    border-width: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    overflow: visible;
}

.k2-slider #unity-dragger-border {
    padding-right: 8px;
    padding-left: 8px;
}

.k2-slider #unity-dragger {
    height: 20px;
    background-color: rgb(140, 148, 163);
    border-color: rgb(20, 22, 28);
    width: 7px;
    margin-top: -10px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    border-width: 1px;
}

.k2-slider #unity-dragger:hover {
    background-color: rgb(170, 178, 195);
}

.k2-slider #unity-dragger:active {
    background-color: rgb(0, 204, 83);
}

.k2-slider #fill_bar {
    display: none;
}

.k2-slider-main {
    border-color: rgb(63, 66, 154);
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-grow: 1;
}

/* Was ">" (direct child) - label_element now lives one level deeper, inside label_row (see
   K2Slider.cs), so this needs to match through that wrapper too. The more specific
   #min_max_bar Label rule below still wins for min/max labels regardless. */
.k2-slider-main Label {
    font-size: 13px;
}

.k2-slider-main #min_max_bar {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: space-between;
}

/* Reese wants these to read like the game's own cockpit gauges: title pinned left, live value
   pinned right in the gauge's amber/yellow instead of both baked into one "Label : value" string
   (which can only have one alignment/color for its whole length). label_element keeps whatever
   default width/margins Unity's BaseField gives it as a title; this just stops it from also
   trying to grow/shrink like a normal field label now that it shares a row with the value. */
.k2-slider-label-row {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    /* Reese: the left (title) side sat flush against the row's own edge with no breathing room,
       while the right (value) side wasn't - reads as lopsided even though both sides are honestly
       just sitting at their natural flex position. Equal padding on both sides guarantees they're
       symmetric regardless of either Label's own default margins. */
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
}

.k2-slider-label-row > Label {
    flex-grow: 0;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    margin: 0;
}

/* Best-guess match to the amber/yellow of the in-game "ORBIT 1969.9 m/s" gauge readout Reese
   referenced - nudge this hex if it's not a close enough match once seen next to the real gauge. */
.k2-slider-value-label {
    color: rgb(242, 201, 76);
}

/* margin-top was -10px, tuned for the old 16px circular dragger. The new dragger is 20px tall
   (see #unity-dragger above) and sits 2px lower around the same center point, so that same -10px
   pull-up was crowding these labels right up against the track - eased off to -2px for more
   clearance. */
.k2-slider-main #min_max_bar Label {
    margin-top: -2px;
    font-size: 11px;
    min-width: 0;
}
